title the shelf learns to take every hand -- the union driver ends the day-index rebase conflict class
prompt Keaton pasted grain-mind's rebase stopping on a conflict in the day-shelf index -- Mind's prepended row against five of Sound's.
think The conflict is structural: every seat prepends its row directly under the shelf's delimiter, so any two clones that both landed laps collide at the same line on every rebase -- the first cured Mind lap hit it on its way home. Two answers. The tree gains a merge=union attribute for session-logs/date/README-index-*.md in the .gitattributes file: the union driver keeps both sides' rows, and since each row is one self-contained line keyed by its own stamp, order is cosmetic and a stamp-order reseat rides on touch (this seat already reseats rows when it finds them out of order). The in-flight rebase gets a one-paste resolution in the reply: checkout --ours takes the upstream shelf, the row is extracted from Mind's own commit with git show and grep, a small python seats it by stamp among the rows, then add, rebase --continue, push both, re-arm.
obs A new day shelf opens with this log -- born on 20260830's own shelf with its index started fresh, per the born-on-its-day law. The union driver reaches every future day index through the glob, so tomorrow's shelves inherit the cure at birth.
loom conflict_class=day_shelf_prepend cure=merge_union rows_kept=both
file .gitattributes the union driver for the day-shelf indexes
file session-logs/date/README-index-20260830.md the new day's shelf index opens
recommend check-in Keaton runs the resolution block; the driver spares every rebase after this one
